<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Network security policy for Hermes Agent.

    Hermes' API server speaks plain HTTP (it does not terminate TLS itself), and
    users point this client at self-hosted instances by raw host:port — a
    loopback 127.0.0.1 (Hermes in Termux on the same phone), a LAN IP such as
    192.168.x.x, or an https:// reverse proxy / tunnel. Android 9+ (API 28+)
    blocks cleartext HTTP by default, which otherwise makes every loopback/LAN
    connection fail with "CLEARTEXT communication not permitted" before a single
    packet leaves the app.

    We therefore permit cleartext app-wide so the common self-hosted setups work
    out of the box.

    SECURITY NOTE: plain HTTP has no transport encryption or authentication of
    the server. On untrusted or public networks, run Hermes behind an HTTPS
    reverse proxy or a private tunnel (Tailscale, WireGuard, SSH) and point the
    app at the https:// address. The bearer token is the only credential
    protecting an endpoint that can run terminal commands — do not send it over
    cleartext across a network you do not control.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />
</network-security-config>
